{"type":"text","timestamp":1775150167783,"sessionID":"ses_2b0ce6b94ffeOjAvmSfn7g6V5v","part":{"id":"prt_d4f31e594001HKIc5NMnv3mNIG","messageID":"msg_d4f31db4a0016tY87rY7z9RBhk","sessionID":"ses_2b0ce6b94ffeOjAvmSfn7g6V5v","type":"text","text":"I've enabled Partial Pre-rendering (PPR) for your Next.js app by updating `next.config.ts` with the `experimental.ppr: true` configuration.\n\n**What this enables:**\n- **Partial Pre-rendering** allows pages to be pre-rendered at build time while still allowing dynamic content to stream in\n- Static parts of the page are cached and served immediately\n- Dynamic parts (using `await`, data fetching, etc.) are streamed in as they resolve\n\n**How to use it:**\n- Wrap dynamic components with React's `Suspense` boundary\n- Use the `loading.js` file for loading states\n- Dynamic components that fetch data will automatically be deferred\n\nThe app is now ready to use PPR. For more granular control, you can also use `ppr: 'incremental'` and opt-in specific routes by exporting a `ppr = true` constant from individual page files.","time":{"start":1775150167782,"end":1775150167782}}}
